  "strategy": "previous_strategy_1: 1. **Approach and rationale**  \n   Start from the parent sequence as it has already proven effective. Use a single phase, stochastic local search optimization that deliberately reduces the sequence length to simplify computations and potentially find lower scores. The primary focus is on aggressively decreasing the sequence length while tracking key convolution metrics. This may uncover lower scores by transitioning to more compact, but higher performance, configurations which are computationally cheaper and converge faster.\n\n2. **Mathematical specification**  \n   The sequence features a length normally decreasing from 800, applied by repeated halving until reaching lengths as brief as 30. Each new sequence is uniquely generated by weighting and applying strong progressions between consecutive indices, inducing precise shifts away from less productive areas and enhancing coordination across the domain.\n\n3. **Implementation procedure**  \n   Initialize with a candidate sequence (if parent available) and then if possible reduce length immediately. Then, iteratively:  \n   a) Check remaining time  \n   b) Select or generate a sequence with a halved length (more ideal for efficiency)  \n   c) Apply critical point mutations and evaluations via explosive gradient jumps  \n   d) Assess the new sequence by floating moment surrogates before acceptance.\n\n4. **Search choices**  \n   Begin with the parent's length or a strategic alternative (if parent not available). Proceed by dividing length by two again and again, ensuring each step re-validates with a complete score.\n\n5. **Time budget**  \n   1500 seconds. Each halving step within a narrow window of time suffices until the evaluation gradients signal no improvement, at which point the optimal return timing starts.\n\n6. **Fallback candidate**  \n   Uniform noise steward optimisation provides a sound fallback. The initial uniform candidate (after clipping) ensures at least a minimal score and safe termination.",
